Title: ECHOES THROUGH GLASS — Paranoid Cut
(15 s, 8K UHD, 30 fps — Psychological Horror / Surreal Realism)

STYLE:
Narrative Arc: paranoia → distortion → rebellion → rupture → silence
Visual Mood: cold silver-blue monochrome; low-key lighting; shallow depth; breathing light, not breathing geometry; reflections feel heavy and reluctant; glass under tension
Setting: bedroom lined with tall, rigid glass mirrors; their surfaces behave like strained solid glass under invisible pressure—distortion appears locally at edges, corners, and points of contact, not as soft global wobble; air remains perfectly dry
Protagonist: @artexmg.evelyn who is dressing in light nightgown; mid 20s; long dark hair; expressive eyes
Antagonist: her distorted reflection that gradually gains autonomy and malevolence; the reflection behaves as if imprisoned behind hard glass, always pushing against it rather than flowing freely

GLOBAL VISUAL / CAMERA / LIGHTING

Render engine: path-traced PBR with caustics and volumetrics
Camera lenses: 35 mm anamorphic (primary), 50 mm macro (close), handheld dolly and orbit shots
Motion blur: 180 degrees shutter / 1⁄48 s exposure
Lens effects: subtle barrel distortion (0.5 %), chromatic fringing (±0.5 %), optical breathing 1 %
Film look: 35 mm LUT, ISO 320, fine grain; halation on speculars

Lighting design: flicker sine 0.5 Hz + strobe bursts 2 Hz + occasional blackout frames
Color palette: black / gunmetal / pale cyan highlights
Environment haze: volumetric density 0.25; absorption 0.6

Mirror behavior:
Mirrors are optically sharp, rigid glass with hard edges and crisp specular highlights.
No soft, rubbery, or jelly-like deformation.
Distortion is localized, asymmetric, and appears only near: frame edges, corners, and where her body, breath, or hands approach.
Motion in the glass is reluctant and delayed, as if the material resists before yielding.
Mirror shader:
Base refractive index 1.52 (realistic glass); during “pull” transition, stress-fracture shears and edge warping occur while refractive index shifts 1.55 → 1.33 along crack lines only.
Brief micro-cracks and stress lines flash for 1–2 frames before any major deformation, then heal.

Distortion hierarchy (very important):
At any moment, prioritize ONE hero effect only:
Either refraction warping OR temporal echo OR chromatic aberration—never all at full strength simultaneously.

Scene Flow (≈2 s each)
[SCENE 1] – Pulse Ignition (0–2 s)
Action:
Extreme macro on her eye; catchlight shows tiny, localized distortions in the mirror edges, pulsing with her heartbeat.
The main mirror plane stays rigid; only thin vertical bands near the frame jitter slightly.
Physics:
Air refractive shimmer IOR ≈ 1.005 around her face only.
Micro-shears confined to mirror edges and highlight streaks, synced to heartbeat amplitude; no full-surface ripples.
Lighting:
Top flicker 1.8 Hz; bloom only on whites; speculars on glass remain sharp, needle-like.
Audio:
Deep sub-bass heartbeat (90 → 100 bpm), reverb tail whisper “wake up” −36 dB.
Mood:

Awareness before panic; the glass feels tense and watchful, not fluid.

[SCENE 2] – Room Awakens (2–4 s)
Action:
She rises slowly.
Before she moves fully, narrow bands of the reflections at the mirror edges begin to shift and shear, as if the glass is under stress.
Distortion spreads in broken vertical streaks, not smooth waves.
Physics:
Droplet simulation (g = −9.8 m/s²) for condensation on the surface only; droplets cling to rigid glass.
Surface “movement” is subtle shear and bending at corners, not global wobble; effective damping ζ = 0.08 (heavy, sluggish response).
Camera:
35 mm handheld pan; rolling-shutter jitter 12 %, emphasizing tension in the hard lines of the mirrors.
Lighting:
Cool morning hue; soft volumetric rays grazing mirror edges and revealing slight refractive bending.
Audio:
Reversed whisper “you’re late”; faint low-freq drone 35 Hz; occasional high, brittle glass creaks panned to the edges.

[SCENE 3] – Echo Lag (4–6 s)
Action:
She turns her head; her reflection moves a fraction of a second late, pressing against the inside of the glass like it’s pushing through a barrier.
Reflection multiplies, but each copy is slightly misaligned and constrained by the mirror frame, as if trapped in panes.
FX:
Temporal ghost frames offset 0.08 s; transparency layering × 4.
Reflections remain sharply contoured, with only subtle edge warping—no rubbery warble across the whole surface.
Physics:
“Mirror fluid tension coefficient 0.4” interpreted as internal stress, not softness: micro-cracks and stress lines flicker briefly where the reflection presses.
Apparent viscosity 0.9 Pa·s only along thin seam lines and stress zones, leaving most of the surface rigid.
Audio:
Heartbeat 110 → 120 bpm; metallic ticking panned L→R; reversed glass chime 0.2 s.
Lighting:
Intermittent blue-white flashes tied to tick rate; flashes catch sharp, knife-like glints along micro-crack lines.

[SCENE 4] – Mirrors Breathe (6–8 s)
Action:
The room “breathes” not by ballooning, but via subtle bowing of the mirror panels between wall studs—millimeter-scale.
Reflections shear and split vertically along hidden stress lines; the geometry stays hard and planar, never soft.
Condensation climbs upward along the glass as if gravity is reversed, beads stretching but still hugging a solid surface.
Physics:
Soft-body bulge amplitude limited to 0.01–0.02 m, anchored to frame edges; deformation feels like bent glass panels, not rubber walls.
Humidity 90 %.
FX:
Fog particle count 50 k; mist turbulence amplitude 0.2; mist flows across the rigid planes, not through them.
Audio:
Wet inhale/exhale with amplitude envelope mapped to bowing cycles of the panels; low-band boost +6 dB at 200 Hz; distant crackle of stressed glass.
Lighting:
Alternating cyan glow / total blackouts every 0.5 s; during glow, hairline fractures briefly visible then fade.

[SCENE 5] – Faces in Glass (8–10 s)
Action:
Multiple desynced reflections encircle her in different mirrors.
Each reflection appears in a separate rigid pane; no mirror behaves like a fluid sheet.
Reflections sometimes “stick” for a frame when she moves, then snap into place, as if constrained by stiff surfaces.
Camera:
Rapid montage, 5 frames per cut, jump-cuts at 12 Hz; framing emphasizes hard rectangular mirror edges.
FX:
Reflection duplication × 4.
Chromatic aberration 0.8 % only at mirror edges and around high-contrast facial features, not across entire frame.
Frame echo 0.1 s on faces, but mirror geometry stays clean and planar.
Audio:
Multi-layer whisper chorus, reverb 6 s, spectral widening 20 %; heartbeat 130 bpm.
Lighting:
Cold under-chin highlights, flicker tied to voice amplitude; reflections hold crisp, knife-like catchlights, reinforcing a glassy feel.
Mood:
Visual/auditory overload; onset of paranoia; the glass feels like a cage, not a liquid medium.

[SCENE 6] – Scream Through Water (10–12 s)
Action:
Her reflection screams silently first, pressing its face hard against the inside of the mirror, flattening slightly but maintaining sharp features.
The sound arrives delayed. The mirror doesn’t wobble; instead, fine radial stress patterns bloom around where her reflected mouth hits the glass.
Physics:
Suspended droplet system (particle radius 1 mm; gravity override −2 m/s²) on the mirror surface and in the air in front of it.
Underwater-style warp refraction appears only inside the reflection’s face and around contact area, not the entire panel.
Ripple frequency 6 Hz strictly localized to a circular zone around the impact.
Audio:
Distorted scream filtered < 1 kHz; heartbeat 140 bpm; wet reverb 70 % wet / 7 s decay.
Lighting:
Strobing white flashes per beat; specular flicker from mirror surface; flashes emphasize hard, jagged reflection contours, not soft smears.
Mood:
Temporal distortion; panic apex.

[SCENE 7] – The Pull (12–14 s)
Action:
The reflection’s arm cracks through the mirror: first, spiderweb fractures radiate from where the hand pushes; then shards dislodge.
As shards break away, their edges briefly liquefy, forming narrow streams that wrap around her arm, dragging her inside.
Her body is yanked toward the mirror; contact points show sharp, splintered edges with thin liquid trails, not fully molten surfaces.
Physics:
Fluid–solid interaction: cohesion 0.4; viscosity 1.2 Pa·s only along fracture edges and flowing shards.
Splash 200 k particles, but streams remain tight and directional, as if constrained by the original solid geometry.
Camera:
Whip-pan transition to half-submerged POV; parallax rotation 20°/s; we briefly see jagged glass silhouettes above, now resealing.
FX:
Refractive displacement map amplitude 0.06 centered around fracture zones; subsurface 0.5 scatter for particle lighting inside the broken plane, not across intact walls.
Audio:
Shatter transient 0 dB peak; Doppler heartbeat pitch −2 semitones; sub-impact 60 Hz −3 dB; short burst of white noise 0.5 s.
Lighting:
Flash-white at moment of break; then murky cyan diffusion within 0.2 s; glints along the remaining sharp edges fade slowly.
Mood:
Violent rupture of reality; glass finally stops resisting and fails.

[SCENE 8] – Submersion Silence (14–15 s)
Action:
Underwater void; her hair floats upward; bubbles rise; above, we see a rigid mirror plane resealing like freezing water—surface tension locked, no wobble
After sealing, the mirror above is perfectly flat and still.
Physics:
Bubble density 0.02; velocity 0.12 m/s; volumetric scatter 0.8, absorption coef 0.7.
Camera:
Slow dolly back; focus breathing; particles drifting in z-depth; the locked, planar mirror surface above remains geometrically perfect.
Lighting:
Dim moon-blue gradient fading to total black; final emphasis on the flat, hard silhouette of the mirror.
Audio:
Heartbeat decelerates → flatline; hiss fades > 500 Hz LP; silence @ 15 s.

Mood:
Tranquil entrapment; after-terror stillness; the world has “reset,” but in a cold, rigid way.
